Fact-Finding Questions (Consulting Services)

Purpose (say up front): Catie asked me to understand the end-to-end MMM onboarding picture, including the Consulting Services part that happens before data reaches DataOps. Goal is a fair, fact-based view of where effort and time actually go across the whole flow. Neutral fact-finding — "unknown" is a fine answer.

Why you: CS is upstream of everything we've mapped so far. Our current baseline effectively starts when data reaches DataOps — your input is what makes it truly end-to-end.

1. The end-to-end timeline (CS → DataOps)
Walk me through what CS does from first customer involvement to the point you hand off to DataOps (requirements, data gathering, cleaning, data dictionary + specs).
From the point CS gets involved to the point DataOps can start running the AIP — how long does that typically take?
Where does the time actually go within the CS portion — requirements, waiting on the customer, data cleaning, spec creation?
What does the handoff to DataOps consist of exactly (data dictionary, specs, cleaned data), and in what format?
2. Worked example — Synchrony Bank
Taking Synchrony Bank (medium complexity, ~20 sources) as an example — how long did the CS portion take, roughly?
For that kind of customer, what were the main things that consumed CS time or caused delay?
3. CS challenges (BAU reality)
What do the typical CS-team challenges look like day to day?
What most often causes delays or rework on the CS side — customer responsiveness, data quality, unclear requirements, something else?
How complete/clean is the data and spec by the time it reaches DataOps? Does anything commonly bounce back?
4. Skills & scope of the CS role
Does the CS team need to understand technical things to do BAU — or is it primarily requirements and customer-facing work?
Where's the line today between what CS does and what DataOps does? Is that line clear, or fuzzy?
CS currently cleans the data — in your view, is that best placed with CS, or would it sit better with the delivery/DataOps teams? (Genuinely after your read, either way.)
5. The customer-facing perspective (general)
From the customer's side, what are the biggest friction points in onboarding?
Where do handoffs between teams (CS → DataOps → modelling) cause the most confusion or delay?
Are there recurring requests or pain points you'd expect leadership to hear about but probably don't?
Does the current platform picture (Meridian / DeciNow) affect CS at all, or is that invisible from where you sit?
If you could change one thing about how onboarding works end to end, what would it be?
6. DDM & platform perspective (their team saw the DDM showcase)

Your team attended a DDM showcase/training a few weeks ago — I'd value the customer-facing read on it, which we don't get from the engineering side.

What was your team's overall impression of DDM from the showcase — did it look like it would help the work you do?
From a CS / customer-facing angle, where do you see DDM helping most? Anywhere it wouldn't help, or adds a step?
Did anything about DDM concern your team, or look not-yet-ready?
Do you feel the current process needs a next-generation platform — or does what we have today basically work for your side?
Is Meridian still usable and adequate from where you sit, or are you hitting real limits with it?
Do you have a sense of whether we actually need to move to DeciNow — or DDM — for the customers you work with? Any scalability issues you've seen firsthand?
Are you aware of TAP (or "Tetris") / any longer-term platform direction? What's your understanding of it, if any?
If a next-gen ingestion platform were built, what would make it genuinely better for the CS-to-delivery handoff?
7. Delivery dates (ask this LAST)
CS currently provides the delivery dates — what are those dates based on? (customer ask, past experience, a formula, DataOps input?)
Do you get DataOps input before a date is committed to the customer?
Do you have a view on whether those dates should be driven by the delivery teams who do the work, rather than set by CS? (Purely your opinion — I'm mapping how it works, not proposing anything.)
Sequencing notes (for you)
Q2, Q5, Q7, Q10, Q12 are the core — the end-to-end time, the SBank example, the challenges, the technical-skill question, and the data-cleaning-ownership question.
DDM/platform (Section 6) comes after the neutral operational questions and before dates — it flows naturally from "how does the work go" into "what did you think of the new tool."
Save dates (Section 7) for last — most sensitive. By then rapport is built, so it lands as curiosity, not a challenge.
Frame the opinion questions (Q12, Q24, Q26, Q31) as "I'd value your read," not "should this change" — you're gathering perspective, not proposing anything.
On reading her perspective (important)
Ask straight questions about DDM, Meridian, DeciNow and next-gen platforms — her views are fair game and useful, and where she leans will surface on its own through honest answers.
Don't fish for her political alignment. If she senses she's being sounded out on where people stand, you lose the trust that makes her a good source and may get a guarded or performed answer. The straight questions above get you the signal without the risk.
Q27 (TAP/Tetris awareness) is a soft probe — if she knows nothing, that's itself a finding (the end-state direction isn't reaching customer-facing teams). Don't push it; just note what she does or doesn't know.
What I'll do with it

Fold the CS portion into the end-to-end baseline (so the timeline is source→stack, not DataOps-only), and capture the handoff and date-ownership observations as factual findings. Attributed to the CS view, kept neutral.
